ANSWERS TO CATEGORY A: COLLECTIONS API & DATA STRUCTURE CHOICES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ANSWER 1 (Map vs. List for Seats):
An `ArrayList<Seat>` requires linear search—checking element by element from index 0
until matching `"Z-999"`, which takes O(N) time. With 50,000 seats and 1,000 threads,
that is up to 50,000,000 comparisons! A `HashMap<String, Seat>` uses the `hashCode()`
of the seat ID string (`"Z-999"`) to jump directly to the memory bucket in O(1)
constant time. Since the seat map is populated once at startup (the coach never grows
new seats mid-journey), reading from `seatInventory.get(seatId)` is lightning fast.

ANSWER 2 (Preventing Duplicate Bookings with Set):
A `HashSet<String>` guarantees uniqueness and checks membership (`confirmedUsers.contains(userId)`)
in O(1) time.
CRITICAL TIMING Nuance:
- You should check `confirmedUsers.contains(userId)` BEFORE locking a seat, AND check
  it again right when finalizing the booking (`confirmedUsers.add(userId)` returns
  `false` if the user was already present!).
- Better yet, you can also maintain a `Set<String> activeLocksByUser` so a single user
  cannot even *lock* two seats simultaneously across two threads.

ANSWER 3 (Waitlist Queue Choice):
A `Queue<String>` (`LinkedList`) enforces strict First-In-First-Out (FIFO) ordering:
`offer(userId)` adds to the tail, and `poll()` removes from the head. A `HashSet`
destroys arrival order, and removing index 0 from an `ArrayList` shifts all remaining
elements in memory.
To prevent a user from entering the waitlist 5 times, before calling
`waitlistQueue.offer(userId)`, check `!waitlistQueue.contains(userId)` (or keep an
accompanying `Set<String> waitlistedUsers` for instant lookup).

ANSWER 4 (Thread Safety of Standard Collections):
If two threads call `auditLogs.add(...)` on an unsynchronized `ArrayList` at the same
millisecond, they may write to the same internal array index, overwriting a log entry,
or trigger an internal array resize simultaneously, throwing a
`ConcurrentModificationException` or `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`.
Solution: Wrap every access to `auditLogs` in a synchronized helper method:
```java
public synchronized void logEvent(String message) {
    auditLogs.add(message);
    System.out.println(message);
}
```
Or use `Collections.synchronizedList(new ArrayList<>())`.


