ANSWERS TO CATEGORY B: MULTITHREADING & SYNCHRONIZATION DESIGN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ANSWER 5 (The Biggest Beginner Trap - Coarse vs. Fine Locking):
If `public synchronized void bookSeat(...)` is synchronized on the whole engine AND
contains `Thread.sleep(1500)`, then the entire booking server turns into a single-file
line! User-2 trying to buy Seat S2 has to wait 1.5 seconds for User-1 to finish
typing their credit card details for Seat S1. In the real world, seats S1 and S2 are
independent resources—locking S1 should only block other people who want S1.

ANSWER 6 (Locking on the Specific Seat Object):
Every Java object has its own intrinsic monitor lock. When you write:
```java
Seat seat = seatInventory.get(seatId);
synchronized (seat) {
    // check and update seat status
}
```
Thread-1 locks the `Seat("S1")` object, while Thread-2 locks the `Seat("S2")` object.
Because those are two distinct objects on the heap, both threads execute their
synchronized blocks simultaneously with zero contention! Meanwhile, Thread-3 trying
to enter `synchronized (seat)` on `Seat("S1")` is paused until Thread-1 exits the
block.

ANSWER 7 (Why Payment Must Happen OUTSIDE the Synchronized Block):
If `Thread.sleep(2500)` happens *inside* `synchronized (seat)`, then Thread-1 holds
the monitor lock on `seat` while sleeping (`Thread.sleep` does NOT release locks!).
When the `LockExpirySweeper` thread wakes up at 2000ms and tries to enter
`synchronized (seat)` to expire the lock, it gets blocked at the door until Thread-1
wakes up at 2500ms! Furthermore, any other user trying to check if S1 is available
will freeze for 2.5 seconds instead of immediately getting a fast `SeatUnavailableException`.
Therefore, you MUST:
1. Enter `synchronized (seat)` -> change status to `LOCKED` -> exit `synchronized (seat)`.
2. Run `Thread.sleep(...)` (Payment) OUTSIDE any lock.
3. Re-enter `synchronized (seat)` -> verify lock is still owned by this user -> set `BOOKED`.

ANSWER 8 (The "Check-Then-Act" Race Condition After Payment):
Timeline of disaster if you don't re-verify ownership in Phase C:
- `0.0s`: User-1 locks Seat S1 (`LOCKED` by User-1) and starts a slow 2.5s payment.
- `2.0s`: `LockExpirySweeper` sees S1 has been locked for 2.0s. It resets S1 to `AVAILABLE`.
- `2.1s`: User-2 locks Seat S1 (`LOCKED` by User-2) and pays in 0.2s -> at `2.3s`, S1 is `BOOKED` by User-2!
- `2.5s`: User-1's slow `Thread.sleep(2500)` finishes! If User-1 blindly sets `S1.status = BOOKED` and `S1.bookedBy = User-1`, User-1 just overwrote User-2's confirmed ticket!
Fix: Inside Phase C's `synchronized (seat)` block, User-1 MUST check:
```java
if (seat.getStatus() != SeatStatus.LOCKED || !userId.equals(seat.getLockedByUserId())) {
    throw new PaymentFailedException("Seat lock expired during payment! Refunding " + userId);
}
```

ANSWER 9 (Daemon Thread vs. Normal Thread for the Sweeper):
The Java Virtual Machine (JVM) stays alive as long as at least one non-daemon ("user")
thread is still running. Since the `LockExpirySweeper` has a `while (true)` loop, a
normal thread will keep the program hanging forever even after `main()` finishes.
Calling `sweeperThread.setDaemon(true)` before `.start()` tells the JVM: "This is a
background helper thread; as soon as `main` and all buyer threads finish, automatically
terminate this thread and exit the program."


