ANSWERS TO CATEGORY C: EXCEPTION HANDLING & FAILURE RECOVERY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ANSWER 10 (State Rollback on Exception):
If an exception is thrown during payment and not handled properly, Seat S1 stays
in the `LOCKED` state until the timeout sweeper cleans it up (or forever if there is
no sweeper). By catching `PaymentFailedException` and immediately releasing the lock,
the seat becomes `AVAILABLE` right away (at 800ms instead of waiting until 2000ms),
allowing the next customer to grab it immediately.

ANSWER 11 (Who Has the Right to Unlock a Seat in the `catch` Block?):
If User-1's `catch` block blindly resets `seat.setStatus(AVAILABLE)` without checking
who currently holds the lock, User-1 will erase User-2's active lock or booking!
Inside the `catch` block, you must synchronize on `seat` and check ownership first:
```java
synchronized (seat) {
    if (seat.getStatus() == SeatStatus.LOCKED && userId.equals(seat.getLockedByUserId())) {
        seat.setStatus(SeatStatus.AVAILABLE);
        seat.setLockedByUserId(null);
        seat.setLockTimestamp(0);
        logEvent("[ROLLBACK] Released lock on " + seatId + " after failed payment by " + userId);
    }
}
```

ANSWER 12 (Checked vs. Unchecked Custom Exceptions):
Extending `Exception` makes them Checked Exceptions. In Java, the compiler forces any
method calling `bookSeat()` to either wrap the call in a `try-catch` block or declare
`throws`. In a financial/booking engine, you want the compiler to force the developer
to consciously handle `SeatUnavailableException` (e.g., by offering the waitlist) and
`PaymentFailedException` (e.g., by triggering a refund alert and lock rollback).


