ANSWERS TO CATEGORY D: WAITLIST & CANCELLATION EDGE CASES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ANSWER 13 (Waitlist Promotion Race Condition):
Never set a cancelled seat to `AVAILABLE` if someone is waiting in the waitlist!
Instead, inside `cancelBooking()`, while holding the lock, check the waitlist. If a
valid waitlisted user exists, keep the seat status as `BOOKED` and directly change
`seat.setBookedByUserId(nextWaitlistedUserId)` without the seat ever becoming
`AVAILABLE` for even a microsecond. That way, no outside thread can steal it!

ANSWER 14 (Ghost Users in the Waitlist):
When polling from `waitlistQueue` during a cancellation, use a `while` loop to skip
anyone who already managed to book another seat in the meantime:
```java
String nextUser = null;
while (!waitlistQueue.isEmpty()) {
    String candidate = waitlistQueue.poll();
    if (!confirmedUsers.contains(candidate)) {
        nextUser = candidate;
        break;
    }
}
```
If `nextUser != null`, promote them! If the loop finishes and `nextUser == null`, then
everyone in the waitlist already got seats elsewhere, so set the seat to `AVAILABLE`.

ANSWER 15 (Preventing Deadlocks Between Two Locks):
A deadlock happens when Thread-1 holds Lock A and waits for Lock B, while Thread-2
holds Lock B and waits for Lock A.
To make deadlocks 100% impossible, choose ONE consistent strategy:
- Strategy 1 (Lock Ordering): Always synchronize on `seat` FIRST, and only call tiny
  `synchronized` helper methods on the engine (`addConfirmedUser`, `pollWaitlist`)
  that immediately return without ever trying to lock a `seat` inside them.
- Since `addConfirmedUser` or `pollWaitlist` never calls `synchronized (seat)`, the
  lock graph only goes in one direction (`Seat Lock -> State Collection Lock`),
  meaning a circular wait (deadlock) can never happen!

ANSWER 16 (Proving Correctness at the End of `main()`):
At the end of `main()`, iterate through all `Seat` objects in `seatInventory.values()`:
1. Count how many seats have `status == SeatStatus.BOOKED` (let's call this `bookedCount`).
2. Put every `seat.getBookedByUserId()` into a temporary `Set<String> uniqueOwners`.
3. Verify three conditions:
   - `bookedCount == uniqueOwners.size()` (Proves no user owns 2 seats).
   - `bookedCount == confirmedUsers.size()` (Proves `confirmedUsers` is 100% in sync with seats).
   - Every seat with `status == AVAILABLE` has `lockedByUserId == null` and `bookedByUserId == null`.
If all three conditions are true after 20 chaotic threads finish fighting over 5 seats,
your concurrency logic is rock solid!